WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.590
Assessment begins by identifying data that must remain confidential for a long time.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:08.160
Teams should look for regulated records, health information, legal material,

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.010
government data, sensitive personal information, authentication secrets,

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:15.895
strategic business information, research and development data, and long-term archives.

00:00:15.895 --> 00:00:20.495
Then they should map the systems that transmit, store, replicate, and back up that data.

00:00:20.495 --> 00:00:23.600
Without that mapping, harvest-now, decrypt-later stays abstract.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.320
The next step is reviewing cryptographic dependencies.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.200
Which TLS endpoints protect the data?

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:33.710
Which VPNs, SSH services, secure email systems, storage products, cloud services, APIs,

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:35.600
and partner integrations are involved?

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.595
Which libraries, certificates, key management systems, hardware security modules,

00:00:39.595 --> 00:00:41.740
and vendor products do those systems rely on?

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:44.800
The answer often spans multiple teams and ownership boundaries,

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.800
which is why this work should not live only in a security spreadsheet.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.800
Prioritization should consider sensitivity, confidentiality lifetime, volume,

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.100
external exposure, mission importance, dependency complexity, and migration difficulty.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.200
A public API carrying sensitive records for long-term retention may deserve earlier

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.200
attention than an internal test endpoint with synthetic data.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.505
A difficult-to-migrate legacy system may also deserve early planning because lead time is

00:01:07.505 --> 00:01:08.300
part of risk.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:12.500
The highest priority is often where value, exposure, and slow migration overlap.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:17.000
Risk assessment should involve security, engineering, legal, privacy, compliance,

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.870
operations, and business stakeholders.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:22.150
Legal and privacy teams can explain data obligations.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.645
Engineering teams can identify real dependencies.

00:01:24.645 --> 00:01:28.400
Operations teams can describe change windows and reliability constraints.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.195
Business owners can clarify impact.

00:01:30.195 --> 00:01:30.490
Together,

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:34.720
they can separate theoretical risk from actionable risk and build a migration roadmap that

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.400
is realistic enough to execute.
